.omo/

# Kilo project config. `.git/info/exclude` already lists `.kilo/`, but that file is
# per-clone and untracked, so a decision recorded in a plan lived only on the machine
# that wrote it. The negations exist because git cannot re-include a file whose
# parent directory is excluded: `.kilo/plans/` re-includes the directory, and
# `.kilo/plans/*.md` re-includes the documents inside it. The same two-step applies to
# every directory re-included here.
#
# **`skills/` and `agent/` are in the repository because gates read them.** Both were
# ignored, and both are read by something under version control:
# `skills-lock.json` at the root describes twenty-one skill directories by upstream and
# content hash, and `scripts/skill-scan.test.mjs` reads `.kilo/skills` to check that the
# lock and the tree agree. With the directory ignored, that test failed on CI with
# "animation-vocabulary is in skills-lock.json but there is no SKILL.md for it" — a gate
# pointed at a directory that is not in the repository, measuring nothing and failing.
# `pnpm skill-scan` scans the prompt surface, so a surface absent from the repository is
# a surface no reviewer ever sees.
#
# `agent/` is here for a sharper version of the same reason: `AGENTS.md` states the
# eleven personas' bodies are the authority for both copies, while `.github/agents/` was
# the only copy under version control. An authority that exists on one machine is not an
# authority.
#
# Worktrees stay out: they are another checkout of this repository, not part of it.
#
# Verified on git 2.55.0, and the negations DO un-ignore the directories themselves, but
# a file *inside* an excluded directory is still reported ignored by `git check-ignore`,
# attributed to `.git/info/exclude`. The documented precedence puts `.gitignore` above
# `$GIT_DIR/info/exclude`; for a path under an excluded directory the exclude file wins
# instead. So on a clone whose `info/exclude` carries the Kilo block — every clone Kilo
# itself has set up — new files here are invisible to `git status` and to `git add -A`,
# and have to be added with `git add -f`. Once tracked they stay tracked; ignore rules
# only govern untracked files. A fresh clone, and CI, have the stock `info/exclude` and
# need no `-f`.
.kilo/*
!.kilo/plans/
!.kilo/plans/*.md
!.kilo/skills/
!.kilo/skills/**
!.kilo/agent/
!.kilo/agent/**

# Coverage and test output
coverage/
test-results/
playwright-report/
blob-report/
.artifacts/
var/

# Vitest cache
.vite/
